+++ b/SoT/SECURITY_MODEL.md
@@ -22,6 +22,8 @@ The Explorer only exposes files allowed by the context security policy. Text edi
 
 The ChatGPT surface is untrusted remote web content. It is isolated from local application privileges by a dedicated `WebContentsView` with sandboxing, context isolation, Node integration disabled, no preload, and a persistent browser partition used only for its web session.
 
+On Linux, the Electron/Chromium process MUST retain a usable layer-1 sandbox. A valid root-owned mode-4755 SUID helper may be used directly (preferably from the persistent Wharness helper path so dependency reinstalls cannot silently remove its privileges). When the packaged helper is not privileged, Wharness may disable only the SUID helper with `--disable-setuid-sandbox` after an actual unprivileged user-namespace probe succeeds; Chromium sandboxing itself remains enabled. `--no-sandbox` is forbidden. If neither a valid SUID helper nor user namespaces are available, desktop startup MUST fail closed with actionable diagnostics.
+
 The Wharness renderer has a separate preload with a narrow allowlisted IPC contract. Remote ChatGPT web content never receives that preload and cannot invoke context-copy or local-workspace APIs. Because a native `WebContentsView` paints above renderer DOM regardless of CSS z-index, trusted Wharness modals that overlap its viewport MUST hide/suspend that native view for the lifetime of the modal and restore it only after the modal closes.
 
 Wharness does not infer authentication from response prose or arbitrary DOM. The persistent Electron partition preserves normal ChatGPT browser state across desktop launches. Authentication is an explicit two-mode boundary. In **Embedded** mode, compatible `chatgpt.com/auth/*` and identity-provider navigation may remain in the sandboxed ChatGPT `WebContentsView`; Node integration stays disabled, context isolation/sandbox/web security stay enabled, and no privileged preload is attached. Google OAuth is not kept in this embedded user-agent because Google policy rejects embedded authorization agents. In **External browser** mode, Wharness cancels the embedded provider navigation and starts a fresh login at `https://chatgpt.com/auth/login` in a real Chrome/Chromium/Brave process using a persistent dedicated Wharness profile under Electron `userData` and a DevTools endpoint bound only to `127.0.0.1`. The dedicated profile is deliberately separate from Chrome's personal/default profile so Chrome 136+ remote-debugging protections are not bypassed; it preserves the Google/ChatGPT account state across Wharness auth attempts. An intercepted in-flight authorization URL is never reused in the external browser.
